@@s001 Linear classifier and logistic regression
@@s002 dato:class +1,due:class −1,ora:least squares line,ok:line chosen by hand
@@s003 The data of the notebook (seed 42): 40 points of class +1 around (1, 1) and 40 of class −1 around (−1, −1). The clouds overlap a little.
@@s004 Least squares on the labels ±1: w = [0.052, 0.386, 0.321]. The boundary is the line w₀ + w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ = 0, that is x₂ = −0.16 − 1.20·x₁. Accuracy <b>0.8875</b>: 9 points out of 80 on the wrong side.
@@s005 On one side wᵀx &gt; 0 and the class is <b>+1</b>, on the other −1. A point is classified well if y · f(x) &gt; 0.
@@s006 The line chosen by hand, w = [0.5, 1, 1] (x₂ = −0.5 − x₁), does better: <b>0.925</b>. Least squares does not count the errors: it minimises the distance from ±1, also of the points already well classified.
@@s007 ok:w = 0.15 b = 3 (notebook),due:w = 0.05,ora:w = 0.5,dato:b = −3,no:boundary σ = 0.5
@@s008 The parameters of the notebook: σ(3 + 0.15x). The curve goes from 0 to 1; it is 0.5 where 3 + 0.15x = 0, that is x = <b>−20</b>: the decision boundary.
@@s009 <b>Small w</b> (0.05): the curve is almost flat, similar to a line; the boundary moves to −3/0.05 = −60.
@@s010 <b>Large w</b> (0.5): almost a step, like sign; boundary −3/0.5 = −6.
@@s011 Changing the <b>bias</b> (b = −3 instead of 3) moves the curve without changing its shape: boundary +20.
@@s012 It is read as a probability: with the parameters of the notebook, at x = 0 σ(3) ≈ 0.95, that is a 95 per cent probability of class 1.
@@s013 dato:class +1,due:class −1,ok:boundary wᵀx = 0
@@s014 dato:class B,due:class A,ora:new point and its neighbours
@@s015 Linear classifier and logistic regression
@@s016 Lecture 9 and the exercises of week 10 (<i>Classification and decision boundaries</i>, <i>Introduction to logistic regression</i>). From regression to classification: the same inner product wᵀx, then an activation function that decides the class. Exam question <b>Q8 (a) and (b)</b>. DW chapter 14.
@@s017 Classifying instead of predicting a number
@@s018 <b>Regression</b>: the output is a number (the price of a house, the position on the screen).
@@s019 <b>Classification</b>: the output is a <b>class</b> (cat / not cat, spam / not spam). Binary: two classes, labels y ∈ {−1, 1} (or {0, 1}).
@@s020 The linear (in fact affine) classifier
@@s021 The same inner product as in regression, with x in homogeneous coordinates [1, x₁, x₂]. With w₀ it is affine, even though it is called "linear".
@@s022 The <b>activation</b> function sign turns the number into a class. (For regression the activation is the identity: z itself.)
@@s023 A line in 2D, a plane in 3D, a hyperplane in n dimensions. On one side +1, on the other −1. The weights decide the slope (w₁/w₂) and the position (w₀).
@@s024 Task 2: if label and prediction have the same sign the product is positive (1·1 or −1·−1); if they are different it is −1.
@@s025 wᵀx, sign and the decision boundary
@@s026 Why different activations, and classifiers can overfit too
@@s027 In regression the output is any number, so the activation is the <b>identity</b>: f(x) = z. In classification the output must be a class: <b>sign</b> gets it from the sign of z, the <b>sigmoid</b> gives a probability between 0 and 1. And yes, a classifier can overfit too: a tree with no depth limit or a k-nearest neighbour with k = 1 gets zero training points wrong because it learns them by heart, but that does not mean they generalise (pages <a href="w10-confini-non-lineari.html">Non-linear boundaries</a> and <a href="w10-knn.html">k-nearest neighbour</a>).
@@s028 Learning w with least squares (Task 4)
@@s029 The decision boundary on the data of the notebook
@@s030 learn_affine_classifier (verified with the data of the notebook, seed 42)
@@s031 The data of the two classes one under the other, and the labels in the same order. Watch out: in the notebook the comment says "-1 for positive class, 0 for negative class", but the text asks for <b>1 and −1</b>: the text counts.
@@s032 Design matrix with the column of 1s (the bias w₀) and least squares: one looks for the w that makes wᵀx as close as possible to ±1.
@@s033 Class = sign. Accuracy 0.8875 (the notebook prints 0.887).
@@s034 Task 5: why by hand it does better (0.925)
@@s035 Least squares does not count the class errors: it measures how far wᵀx is from ±1. A point classified very well, with wᵀx = 1.62, "errs" just the same by (1.62 − 1)² = 0.38 and pulls the boundary towards itself. That is why w = [0.5, 1, 1], chosen by eye, reaches 0.925. For classification one needs losses designed for the purpose: the logistic one (below) or the hinge of the SVMs.
@@s036 Logistic regression
@@s037 Three pieces (Tasks 1–3)
@@s038 A linear function of the input.
@@s039 The <b>sigmoid</b> squeezes any number between 0 and 1: it is read as the <b>probability</b> that x is of class 1.
@@s040 σ(0) = 0.5: the decision boundary is again w₀ + w₁x = 0, the same as the linear classifier. With the parameters of the notebook [3, 0.15]: x = −3/0.15 = <b>−20</b> (σ(−20·0.15 + 3) = 0.5, verified).
@@s041 If you change…
@@s042 the sigmoid…
@@s043 small w₁
@@s044 it becomes almost flat, similar to a line: the probabilities change slowly
@@s045 large w₁
@@s046 it becomes almost a step: it approaches sign
@@s047 it is constant: the probability does not depend on x
@@s048 the bias w₀
@@s049 it shifts to the right or to the left, that is the boundary moves
@@s050 How the parameters change the sigmoid
@@s051 How it is trained: the logistic loss (log loss, binary cross-entropy)
@@s052 For a point of class 1 only −log(p) counts.
@@s053 Little loss if you are sure and right, a great deal if you are sure and wrong. Unlike least squares, a point "very right" is not punished.
@@s054 Like a weather app. For a point of class 0 only −log(1 − p) counts. Saying "99 per cent chance of rain" and staying dry costs a great deal; saying "10 per cent" and staying dry costs little. The loss rewards being sure when you are right and punishes being sure when you are wrong.
@@s055 The sigmoid makes the problem non-linear in the weights: one starts from a w and improves it in small steps (week 14). With the data of Task 4, sklearn's <code>LogisticRegression</code> finds w₀ = 0.205, w = [1.791, 1.537], accuracy 0.8875.
@@s056 Why probabilities are useful
@@s057 A probability also says <b>how</b> sure the model is. Changing the threshold 0.5 exchanges false positives and false negatives (week 11): in medicine one lowers the threshold so as not to miss sick people.
@@s058 Accuracy 0.04 in a two-class problem
@@s059 In Task 5 of the notebook "accuracy 0.040" appears. With two classes, an accuracy far below 0.5 almost always means <b>inverted labels</b> (the model says 1 where the truth is 0): the classifier is excellent (0.96) but compared with the swapped classes.
@@s060 k-nearest neighbour (lecture 9)
@@s061 A new point takes the class of the <b>majority of its k neighbours</b> in the training. It learns no weights: it remembers all the data (memory-based) and measures distances, so the choice of the <a href="w02-norme-distanze.html">distance</a> and the scale of the features matter. k = 1 gives accuracy 1.0 on the training (each point is its own neighbour): it says nothing about generalisation. Pros: no training, boundaries of any shape. Cons: slow to predict with many data, sensitive to unscaled features and to useless ones. The exercise of week 10, with the ties, the four dimensions and the comparison with the line, is on the page <a href="w10-knn.html">k-nearest neighbour: the exercise</a>.
@@s062 Q8 (a) and (b)
@@s063 (a) "Linear classification, kernels, and classification boundaries." (b) "Logistic regression and classification boundaries." To bring: wᵀx and the sign, the boundary as a hyperplane, y·f(x) &gt; 0, least squares on the labels and its limit (Task 5), sigmoid and probability, boundary at σ = 0.5, log loss and gradient descent.
@@s064 k = 1, 3, 5: the answer changes
